I'd build it in two layers. The first one does most of the work and needs no new infrastructure.

## Why nothing is cached today

- The CDN is already in front of the app. It follows whatever `Cache-Control` the app sends, and platform engineering won't add path rules (`CDN.md`).
- `middleware/security.js:8` sends `no-store, no-cache, must-revalidate, private` on every response, including `/p`. That's why the hit rate is 0%.
- That header was the fix for an audit finding (an account page was served to the wrong user from a cache). It was applied to the whole app to meet a deadline, not because product pages needed it.
- The product route's own comments say it reads no session and nothing on it varies by user (`routes/products.js:6-8`).

## Layer 1: let the CDN cache product pages

Keep `no-store` as the default for every route, and have `/p` opt out only when it succeeds. `/account` and any future route stay uncacheable unless someone deliberately changes that, so the audit fix still holds.

```js
// routes/products.js (sketch)
router.get('/:slug', async (req, res) => {
  const product = await db.productBySlug(req.params.slug);
  if (!product) return res.status(404).send('not found');   // keeps no-store
  const [related, stock] = await Promise.all([
    db.relatedProducts(product.id),
    db.stockFor(product.id),
  ]);
  res.set('Cache-Control', 'public, max-age=60, s-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=600');
  res.removeHeader('Pragma');
  res.render('product', { product, related, stock });
});
```

- **Set the header only when the page succeeds.** 404s and errors keep `no-store`, so a missing product or an error page never gets cached.
- **Browser time vs CDN time.** `max-age` is how long browsers keep the page, and you can't clear a browser's copy, so keep it short. `s-maxage` is how long the CDN keeps it.
- **`stale-while-revalidate`** lets the CDN serve the old page while it fetches a new one in the background. That way a busy product never makes users wait 900ms when its cache entry expires.
- **Stock is what limits the timings.** Admin edits happen a few times a day, but stock changes whenever something sells. The page can show stock up to about `s-maxage` + `stale-while-revalidate` old. If that's too stale, cache the page longer and load stock from a separate small endpoint (e.g. `/p/:slug/stock`) with `max-age=10` or no caching.
- **Don't add `Vary: Cookie`.** The page doesn't depend on cookies, and it would split the cache per visitor.

**Guardrails.** Since this touches an audit fix, whoever owns that finding should probably sign off.
- **No cookies on `/p` responses.** The CDN doesn't strip cookies, so a cached page that sets a cookie would hand that cookie to every visitor. `account.js` reads `req.session`, but no session middleware is registered in `app.js`. Wherever it's added, it has to apply only to `/account`, not the whole app.
- **Add header tests:**
  - `/p/<real>` returns `public`
  - `/p/<missing>` returns `no-store`
  - `/account/orders` returns `no-store, private` and nothing cacheable
- **Leave a comment in the route** saying that showing anything user-specific on the page means removing the cache header.

## Layer 2: cache the slow queries inside the app

A CDN miss (a rarely viewed product, each CDN location's first request, or right after expiry) still takes the full database time. A small in-process cache handles that:

```js
// sketch: caches the promise, so simultaneous misses share one query
function cached(cache, key, ttlMs, load) {
  const hit = cache.get(key);
  if (hit && hit.expires > Date.now()) return hit.value;
  const value = load().catch(err => { cache.delete(key); throw err; });
  cache.set(key, { value, expires: Date.now() + ttlMs });
  return value;
}
```

- **`relatedProducts` (~600ms)** benefits most, because tags and products rarely change. Cache it by product id for minutes to an hour.
- **`productBySlug` (~40ms)** is cheap, but caching it is harmless.
- **`stockFor` (~250ms)** should get a TTL of seconds, or not be cached at all.
- **Use a size-limited cache** such as `lru-cache` rather than a plain `Map`. If you run many instances and want them to share a cache, use Redis, but start in-process.
- **Clearing entries on admin edits:** a timeout is the simplest option. If the admin tool is a separate service, it can't clear this app's memory anyway. If you need changes to show up immediately, a Postgres `LISTEN/NOTIFY` trigger can tell the app to drop the entry.

## Two fixes that aren't caching but reduce misses

- **Run the two slow queries at the same time.** They're independent, so `Promise.all` (shown above) takes an uncached page from about 890ms to about 640ms.
- **Look at the `relatedProducts` query itself.** 600ms is a lot for `limit 8`. First check for indexes on `product_tags(product_id)` and `product_tags(tag_id)`. The join to `products p2` isn't needed, because `pt2.product_id = $1` does the same thing. Without `DISTINCT`, a product that shares several tags can appear more than once.

I didn't edit or run anything. The timings come from the comments in `db.js`, not from measurements.
